<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<KeyValueMapOperations continueOnError="true" enabled="true" name="KVM-LoadRuntimeConfig" mapIdentifier="llm-gateway-config">
    <DisplayName>Load runtime configuration</DisplayName>
    <!--
      Runtime switches, Agent Platform target shape, allowed models, per-call limits and
      the fail mode. Stored as one JSON document under a single key.

      WHY A KVM AND NOT PROXY PROPERTIES
      ==================================
      Everything in here is something an operator may need to change under time
      pressure: raise an output ceiling, drop a model from the allowlist, flip
      enforcement off during an incident. A KVM edit takes effect within the cache
      window; a proxy property change requires a bundle rebuild, a redeploy and a
      revision bump. Configuration that is expensive to change does not get
      changed - it gets worked around.

      expiryTimeInSecs is the propagation delay for a config edit. 300s trades a
      five-minute lag for a very large reduction in KVM reads. Lower it if you
      need faster rollback; see docs/12-operations-runbook.md.

      continueOnError is true so that a KVM read failure reaches
      JS-BuildIdentityKeys, which turns it into an explicit, legible 503 rather
      than an opaque policy fault. Missing configuration is always fail-closed:
      traffic that cannot be priced is not served.
    -->
    <Scope>environment</Scope>
    <ExpiryTimeInSecs>300</ExpiryTimeInSecs>
    <Get assignTo="cap.raw.runtimeConfig" index="1">
        <Key>
            <Parameter>runtime-config</Parameter>
        </Key>
    </Get>
</KeyValueMapOperations>
